WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:03.292
- I had a sort of secret
desire as a high schooler

00:00:03.292 --> 00:00:04.463
to be a comedian.

00:00:04.463 --> 00:00:05.598
I didn't know what that meant.

00:00:05.598 --> 00:00:08.597
There is no secret society out there,

00:00:08.597 --> 00:00:10.733
that will tap you on
the shoulder one night

00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:11.909
and show you the way.

00:00:11.909 --> 00:00:14.808
We're working really hard
to make jokes about ideas.

00:00:14.808 --> 00:00:16.771
The lawyer for Google

00:00:16.771 --> 00:00:19.181
would read these statements
that I said on air

00:00:19.181 --> 00:00:20.787
and I said, well I didn't say that.

00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:25.220
- He's an American comedian,
writer, actor, and tv host.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.485
He currently hosts a late night
television talk show on CBS.

00:00:28.485 --> 00:00:31.320
His book, I am American and So Can You,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.246
was number one on the New
York Times, best seller list.

00:00:34.246 --> 00:00:35.281
He's Stephen Colbert

00:00:35.281 --> 00:00:38.548
and here is my take on his
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:38.548 --> 00:00:40.003
Rule number six is my personal favorite

00:00:40.003 --> 00:00:41.549
and make sure sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:42.464
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:42.464 --> 00:00:43.895
Also, as Stephen is talking,

00:00:43.895 --> 00:00:45.545
if he says something
that really inspires you,

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.492
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:47.492 --> 00:00:48.704
and put quotes around it

00:00:48.704 --> 00:00:50.729
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:50.729 --> 00:00:53.312
(upbeat music)

00:01:03.882 --> 00:01:05.550
- We'd only been on 32.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.556
I think we hadn't even done 50 shows.

00:01:07.556 --> 00:01:08.577
- Wow.

00:01:08.577 --> 00:01:11.559
- And so, I call Jon Stewart and said,

00:01:11.559 --> 00:01:15.726
Jon, I just got invited for
the correspondents dinner,

00:01:19.676 --> 00:01:21.304
do you think I should do it?

00:01:21.304 --> 00:01:23.638
And he said, what, to like attend?

00:01:23.638 --> 00:01:25.832
And I said, no, they want me.

00:01:25.832 --> 00:01:27.633
'cause they want you to be the guy?

00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:29.211
Like to stand next to the president?

00:01:29.211 --> 00:01:30.512
Do they know what you do?

00:01:30.512 --> 00:01:31.803
And I said, I don't know.

00:01:31.803 --> 00:01:34.110
And I said, I think I have to do it.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.320
You have to do it!

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.437
Like right now, you have to do it.

00:01:37.437 --> 00:01:40.714
So don't pay attention to
the approval ratings that say

00:01:40.714 --> 00:01:44.393
68% of Americans disapprove of the job

00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:45.758
this man is doing.

00:01:45.758 --> 00:01:47.226
I ask you this,

00:01:47.226 --> 00:01:48.997
does not that also logically mean

00:01:48.997 --> 00:01:52.997
that 68% approve of
the job he's not doing?

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:56.253
(audience laughing)

00:01:56.253 --> 00:01:58.420
Think about it, I haven't.

00:02:01.436 --> 00:02:03.850
- Why did comedy kind of become
of your thing, do you think?

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.789
Or did comedy kind of pick you?

00:02:06.789 --> 00:02:08.350
- Well that's a nice way of putting it.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.574
That's a nice way of putting it.

00:02:09.574 --> 00:02:10.574
I would say,

00:02:12.555 --> 00:02:14.472
I can't under emphasize

00:02:17.297 --> 00:02:20.653
how important comedy's been to my life

00:02:20.653 --> 00:02:22.828
and how important certain opportunities

00:02:22.828 --> 00:02:24.743
that came along to my life

00:02:24.743 --> 00:02:26.327
and they all, many of them
seem sort of accidental.

00:02:26.327 --> 00:02:27.941
For instance, like second city,

00:02:27.941 --> 00:02:30.117
I didn't think I was
going to be a comedian.

00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:32.763
I had a sort of secret
desire as a high schooler

00:02:32.763 --> 00:02:33.766
to be a comedian.

00:02:33.766 --> 00:02:35.119
I didn't know what that meant.

00:02:35.119 --> 00:02:37.182
You know, I just really liked being funny

00:02:37.182 --> 00:02:38.569
and from one of 11 kids

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:40.200
and we're a funny family

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.617
and being funny is important.

00:02:43.693 --> 00:02:46.034
The king of the room was who
ever was funniest, you know?

00:02:46.034 --> 00:02:48.201
And I remember as a child,

00:02:50.473 --> 00:02:53.500
I seen comedy help, my
family had a tragedy

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.333
when I was younger,

00:02:54.333 --> 00:02:55.632
my father and two of my brothers died

00:02:55.632 --> 00:02:58.215
and I remember my sister making

00:02:59.422 --> 00:03:00.921
another one of my sisters

00:03:00.921 --> 00:03:02.356
laugh so hard on the way,

00:03:02.356 --> 00:03:05.655
the car away from the cemetery.

00:03:05.655 --> 00:03:07.956
One of my sisters made the other sister

00:03:07.956 --> 00:03:11.123
laugh so hard that she fell
on the floor of the limo.

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:12.273
When of those big floors

00:03:12.273 --> 00:03:13.830
with the rumble seat facing each other

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.242
and she fell on the floor laughing

00:03:15.242 --> 00:03:16.649
and I remember thinking,

00:03:16.649 --> 00:03:17.942
I want to do that.

00:03:17.942 --> 00:03:19.475
And I don't know whether
it was specifically

00:03:19.475 --> 00:03:21.008
when the context of dealing with tragedy

00:03:21.008 --> 00:03:22.771
because I don't think, I was also 10,

00:03:22.771 --> 00:03:24.508
but I remember specifically thinking,

00:03:24.508 --> 00:03:27.260
I want that, I would have loved

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.633
to been able to do that right now

00:03:28.633 --> 00:03:32.874
and then I fell asleep
every night for years

00:03:32.874 --> 00:03:37.041
listening to Bill Cosby,
Wonderfulness, Very Funny Fellow,

00:03:38.941 --> 00:03:40.931
David Frye, Richard Nixon, A Fantasy,

00:03:40.931 --> 00:03:43.573
George Carlin and Class Clown,

00:03:43.573 --> 00:03:46.573
Let's Get Small, Wild and Crazy Guy,

00:03:48.026 --> 00:03:49.438
back when you could stack albums.

00:03:49.438 --> 00:03:52.856
Stack so many of them, the
top one kind of played slow

00:03:52.856 --> 00:03:54.557
as it went around

00:03:54.557 --> 00:03:57.595
and then I went to college to be an actor.

00:03:57.595 --> 00:03:59.026
But an actor actor.

00:03:59.026 --> 00:04:00.621
I wore black and I had a beard

00:04:00.621 --> 00:04:03.371
and you know, I was like, let
me share my misery with you.

00:04:03.371 --> 00:04:04.946
(audience laughing)

00:04:04.946 --> 00:04:07.850
Poets slash jerk kind of actor.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:09.484
And then I accidentally met
some people from second city

00:04:09.484 --> 00:04:11.106
and took some classes there

00:04:11.106 --> 00:04:12.474
and got invited to audition

00:04:12.474 --> 00:04:13.794
and accidentally.

00:04:13.794 --> 00:04:15.138
Just sort of a happy accident,

00:04:15.138 --> 00:04:16.976
fell in with some great people

00:04:16.976 --> 00:04:19.689
and I still, I quit second city four times

00:04:19.689 --> 00:04:22.297
in order to go do straight black box,

00:04:22.297 --> 00:04:24.849
avant garde kind of theater in Chicago.

00:04:24.849 --> 00:04:25.930
Which that was what I was going to be.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.702
I was going to live in a studio apartment

00:04:27.702 --> 00:04:30.660
with no furniture and
a futon on the floor,

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.688
that I stuffed myself with yak fur

00:04:32.688 --> 00:04:35.105
and just drank and be single,

00:04:35.939 --> 00:04:39.891
with the beard and you
know, sandals and a dashiki

00:04:39.891 --> 00:04:43.197
and I was going to drink from a san malvar

00:04:43.197 --> 00:04:46.929
that was constantly
bubbling in the background.

00:04:46.929 --> 00:04:48.109
But one day I was back stage

00:04:48.109 --> 00:04:50.025
and I kept on returning to do comedy

00:04:50.025 --> 00:04:51.770
and I was backstage one night

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.072
and this is really the thing
that made the decision for me.

00:04:54.072 --> 00:04:56.237
I was backstage with a
guy named Dave Risowski,

00:04:56.237 --> 00:04:57.154
who does a,

00:05:00.966 --> 00:05:02.383
what's it called?

00:05:05.513 --> 00:05:07.165
He does podcast, does a great podcast.

00:05:07.165 --> 00:05:10.420
What do you kids do, your podcasts?

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:11.438
He's a great guy.

00:05:11.438 --> 00:05:13.024
We're backstage, somebody was on stage

00:05:13.024 --> 00:05:16.662
and they were supposed to
do a very simple blackout.

00:05:16.662 --> 00:05:18.069
And a blackout is a very short,

00:05:18.069 --> 00:05:19.531
it's got one joke and
then the lights go out.

00:05:19.531 --> 00:05:20.787
This is second city.

00:05:20.787 --> 00:05:22.408
It's a pace keeper for the show.

00:05:22.408 --> 00:05:24.218
She goes out there and
the blackout is this,

00:05:24.218 --> 00:05:25.827
she goes, you know.

00:05:25.827 --> 00:05:26.954
You're supposed to say,

00:05:26.954 --> 00:05:28.434
I'd like to do a song for you now.

00:05:28.434 --> 00:05:30.556
Welcome to the no exit cafe.

00:05:30.556 --> 00:05:32.432
I'd like to do a song for you right now.

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.018
A song for the whales.

00:05:34.018 --> 00:05:37.317
You tune up your guitar for a long time.

00:05:37.317 --> 00:05:39.369
This is a song for the whales.

00:05:39.369 --> 00:05:40.372
And then you go,

00:05:40.372 --> 00:05:44.192
(terrible whale noises)

00:05:44.192 --> 00:05:47.802
very simple, you do whistling
and clicks and everything.

00:05:47.802 --> 00:05:50.614
It's fine, not a big, not a great laugh

00:05:50.614 --> 00:05:51.699
but it works every time.

00:05:51.699 --> 00:05:53.282
She goes out there to do it.

00:05:53.282 --> 00:05:55.500
I'd like to do a song for you right now.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.611
I'd like to do a song for you right now.

00:05:57.611 --> 00:05:59.351
She goes into her
whistling and her clicking

00:05:59.351 --> 00:06:01.457
and we're backstage waiting to go on

00:06:01.457 --> 00:06:03.078
for the next scene, me and Dave,

00:06:03.078 --> 00:06:05.966
and we're not getting any laughs.

00:06:05.966 --> 00:06:07.425
This is foolproof.

00:06:07.425 --> 00:06:08.997
No laughs at all, what's going on?

00:06:08.997 --> 00:06:10.685
Something's wrong.

00:06:10.685 --> 00:06:11.741
And then she goes,

00:06:11.741 --> 00:06:14.002
oh I forgot, it's song for whales.

00:06:14.002 --> 00:06:17.002
(audience laughing)

00:06:20.209 --> 00:06:23.896
And we burst into laughter backstage

00:06:23.896 --> 00:06:27.073
and we threw our arms around each other

00:06:27.073 --> 00:06:29.406
in the agony of her failure.

00:06:31.804 --> 00:06:33.514
And we're just laughing.

00:06:33.514 --> 00:06:36.783
We fell like a collapsing teepee.

00:06:36.783 --> 00:06:38.225
We just fell to the ground

00:06:38.225 --> 00:06:42.023
and Dave's feet went out
onto stage like this,

00:06:42.023 --> 00:06:44.856
as we held each other like lovers.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:49.382
The most intimate, joyful
experience at her pain,

00:06:49.382 --> 00:06:50.385
that we all knew

00:06:50.385 --> 00:06:51.786
and she could hear it happen

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:53.001
and the audience could see our feet

00:06:53.001 --> 00:06:55.448
and she started laughing at how

00:06:55.448 --> 00:06:57.981
wonderfully she had just failed.

00:06:57.981 --> 00:07:00.511
And I thought at that moment,

00:07:00.511 --> 00:07:01.955
this is what I want.

00:07:01.955 --> 00:07:03.955
If failure of this scale

00:07:05.263 --> 00:07:08.180
can cause this much joy for anyone,

00:07:09.476 --> 00:07:12.130
than this is the healthiest
thing that I could do

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.125
with the rest of my life

00:07:13.125 --> 00:07:14.475
and I will do nothing else

00:07:14.475 --> 00:07:17.677
and I have never looked
back from that moment.

00:07:17.677 --> 00:07:18.688
- What is the goal?

00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:20.067
In your mind, what was the
goal of political satire?

00:07:20.067 --> 00:07:21.634
- To make people laugh.

00:07:21.634 --> 00:07:24.447
You know, I would make
jokes about politics

00:07:24.447 --> 00:07:26.033
because my model was the news

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:27.588
and I care about politics

00:07:27.588 --> 00:07:30.436
and I care about current events

00:07:30.436 --> 00:07:31.888
and I'll always do jokes about that.

00:07:31.888 --> 00:07:33.753
I mean, the new show has that as well.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:36.171
We're definitely, that's
big part of the new show,

00:07:36.171 --> 00:07:37.713
especially going into a
presidential election.

00:07:37.713 --> 00:07:40.796
But the goal is to make people laugh.

00:07:42.073 --> 00:07:43.914
I mean, politics is
incredibly frustrating,

00:07:43.914 --> 00:07:45.360
the news can be incredibly depressing.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.309
If we just make your
day a little bit better,

00:07:47.309 --> 00:07:49.059
you know, at the end,

00:07:50.084 --> 00:07:53.101
because we make jokes that show,

00:07:53.101 --> 00:07:55.045
yeah, we feel the same way you do or like,

00:07:55.045 --> 00:07:56.642
or rather we curate for you,

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:58.517
our feelings about the news

00:07:58.517 --> 00:08:01.991
and maybe you agree with our jokes

00:08:01.991 --> 00:08:03.588
or what's funny about the day's news.

00:08:03.588 --> 00:08:05.890
That sense of connection between

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:08.540
the performer and the audience
is the entire intention.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:10.047
You know, why does anybody,

00:08:10.047 --> 00:08:11.964
what does anybody want?

00:08:12.876 --> 00:08:13.943
Not to be alone

00:08:13.943 --> 00:08:15.737
and I think a performer gets on stage

00:08:15.737 --> 00:08:17.084
and says the things that are in his mind,

00:08:17.084 --> 00:08:18.498
in is own particular way,

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:21.240
to me a connection with an audience

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.043
so he doesn't feel so alone.

00:08:23.043 --> 00:08:26.404
And hopefully the audience
feels the same way.

00:08:26.404 --> 00:08:29.737
It's not conscious, it's here, not here.

00:08:32.265 --> 00:08:34.208
And so that's got to be the goal.

00:08:34.208 --> 00:08:35.729
That connection's got to be the goal

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:39.896
and the making somebody
laugh has got to be the goal.

00:08:41.087 --> 00:08:45.254
You can't think that you're
satire is going to change things.

00:08:46.184 --> 00:08:47.359
Peter Cook famously said,

00:08:47.359 --> 00:08:50.942
a great satirist, worked
with Dudley Moore,

00:08:53.526 --> 00:08:55.487
and in beyond the fringe,

00:08:55.487 --> 00:08:59.163
he famously said that the greatest satire

00:08:59.163 --> 00:09:00.491
of the 20th century,

00:09:00.491 --> 00:09:01.829
was the Vimart Caberet

00:09:01.829 --> 00:09:04.083
and boy, they just stopped
Hitler in their tracks.

00:09:04.083 --> 00:09:06.493
You know, you can't imagine,

00:09:06.493 --> 00:09:08.381
however great your joke is,

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:11.254
that it's going to change things.

00:09:11.254 --> 00:09:12.504
What it does is

00:09:14.264 --> 00:09:16.110
just make a connection with any audience

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.566
who's feeling the same way you do.

00:09:17.566 --> 00:09:21.243
While traditional paths
may seem harder to find,

00:09:21.243 --> 00:09:24.144
that also means that you may learn,

00:09:24.144 --> 00:09:26.030
sooner than most generations,

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:28.447
the hard lesson that you must

00:09:29.594 --> 00:09:31.192
always make the path for yourself.

00:09:31.192 --> 00:09:34.275
There is no secret society out there,

00:09:35.625 --> 00:09:37.543
that will tap you on
the shoulder one night

00:09:37.543 --> 00:09:38.958
and show you the way.

00:09:38.958 --> 00:09:40.873
Because the true secret is,

00:09:40.873 --> 00:09:42.734
your life will not be defined

00:09:42.734 --> 00:09:45.413
by the society we have left you.

00:09:45.413 --> 00:09:47.580
To paraphrase Robert Bolt,

00:09:48.483 --> 00:09:51.208
society has no more idea of what you are,

00:09:51.208 --> 00:09:52.914
than what you do.

00:09:52.914 --> 00:09:57.361
Because ultimately, it has
only your brains to think with.

00:09:57.361 --> 00:09:59.993
Every generation must define itself

00:09:59.993 --> 00:10:03.071
and so make the world that suits itself.

00:10:03.071 --> 00:10:05.904
So, if you must find your own path

00:10:08.745 --> 00:10:11.578
and we have left you no easy path,

00:10:12.992 --> 00:10:16.108
then decide now to choose the hard path

00:10:16.108 --> 00:10:18.775
that leads to the life and
the world that you want.

00:10:18.775 --> 00:10:23.558
In my experience, you will
truly serve only what you love

00:10:23.558 --> 00:10:26.641
because service is love made visible.

00:10:28.038 --> 00:10:32.293
If you love friends, you
will serve your friends.

00:10:32.293 --> 00:10:36.193
If you love community, you
will serve your community.

00:10:36.193 --> 00:10:39.520
If you love money, you
will serve your money.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:41.693
And if you love only yourself,

00:10:41.693 --> 00:10:44.132
you will serve only yourself

00:10:44.132 --> 00:10:46.382
and you will have only yourself.

00:10:46.382 --> 00:10:48.299
So no winning, instead,

00:10:50.289 --> 00:10:53.151
try to love others and serve others

00:10:53.151 --> 00:10:54.647
and hopefully find those

00:10:54.647 --> 00:10:57.818
that will love and serve you in return.

00:10:57.818 --> 00:10:59.987
- I was just wondering
if you had anything left

00:10:59.987 --> 00:11:01.571
on your career wishlist?

00:11:01.571 --> 00:11:05.238
- Sure, I never know
what's going to happen,

00:11:06.533 --> 00:11:08.575
you know, even tomorrow.

00:11:08.575 --> 00:11:10.027
I just got to spend the week

00:11:10.027 --> 00:11:14.258
with some of my favorite
creative artists with The Hobbit.

00:11:14.258 --> 00:11:15.590
It was unbelievable, what a workshop.

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:16.933
Made my own hobbit feet.

00:11:16.933 --> 00:11:21.779
Next week, I get to sing with
all these wonderful artists,

00:11:21.779 --> 00:11:23.394
doing Christmas carols.

00:11:23.394 --> 00:11:26.854
I've done my own Christmas special.

00:11:26.854 --> 00:11:28.023
I've had a rally on the mall.

00:11:28.023 --> 00:11:30.558
I've testified before congress.

00:11:30.558 --> 00:11:34.198
I may or may not appear in The Hobbit.

00:11:34.198 --> 00:11:36.152
(audience laughing)

00:11:36.152 --> 00:11:39.087
I get to, the nice thing about my show,

00:11:39.087 --> 00:11:40.914
and whether or not I do the show forever,

00:11:40.914 --> 00:11:42.256
I mean everything ends.

00:11:42.256 --> 00:11:43.692
But whether or not I do the show forever,

00:11:43.692 --> 00:11:45.049
the nice thing about my show,

00:11:45.049 --> 00:11:46.810
as the host and executive producer,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.554
I get to ask of myself,

00:11:48.554 --> 00:11:51.456
anything that I want to try.

00:11:51.456 --> 00:11:55.690
And but that also means I
have to do everything I know.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:59.013
And so, it's just this tremendous
sort of refreshing gift.

00:11:59.013 --> 00:12:00.745
As tiring as the show sort of is.

00:12:00.745 --> 00:12:04.495
'cause I'm kind of
decaying before your eyes.

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:09.521
It's in the same way,
it's also rejuvenating.

00:12:09.521 --> 00:12:13.017
The show is only what I want it to be.

00:12:13.017 --> 00:12:14.550
I can always say no to myself

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:16.321
or ask myself something new.

00:12:16.321 --> 00:12:17.332
But beyond this,

00:12:17.332 --> 00:12:19.268
I just want to work with people I like.

00:12:19.268 --> 00:12:20.671
I love the people I work with,

00:12:20.671 --> 00:12:21.735
I love what we do

00:12:21.735 --> 00:12:23.321
and I just want to be
able to do it with joy

00:12:23.321 --> 00:12:24.506
and the moment I can't do that,

00:12:24.506 --> 00:12:26.664
I got to stop and try
and do something else.

00:12:26.664 --> 00:12:27.660
You and I have both hosted

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:28.925
the White House correspondents dinner.

00:12:28.925 --> 00:12:31.007
- Yes, and I have to say I
was very inspired by Stephen.

00:12:31.007 --> 00:12:32.829
When you called President
Bush your nigger,

00:12:32.829 --> 00:12:36.203
(audience laughing)

00:12:36.203 --> 00:12:37.773
I could not believe you did that, Stephen.

00:12:37.773 --> 00:12:38.980
- Well, very few people
appreciated the courage

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.782
that took me to say it.

00:12:40.782 --> 00:12:42.083
- I thought it was very brave.

00:12:42.083 --> 00:12:44.359
I kind of stole it from you.

00:12:44.359 --> 00:12:46.449
I just wanted to give you props man.

00:12:46.449 --> 00:12:48.549
- You got it, baby,
you got it, you got it.

00:12:48.549 --> 00:12:50.132
- You got it, baby!

00:12:52.478 --> 00:12:54.421
- That is a really tough room.

00:12:54.421 --> 00:12:55.822
What people at home don't understand

00:12:55.822 --> 00:12:57.109
is that all they ever see on TV

00:12:57.109 --> 00:12:59.231
is like the dais and a couple
people in the front row.

00:12:59.231 --> 00:13:02.183
It's 3000 people in
there, it's a huge room.

00:13:02.183 --> 00:13:03.197
Do you know Harry J. Lennix?

00:13:03.197 --> 00:13:04.689
Do you know who Harry is?

00:13:04.689 --> 00:13:05.522
Harry is --

00:13:05.522 --> 00:13:06.355
- Oh, actor!

00:13:06.355 --> 00:13:07.188
- He's an actor.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:13:07.188 --> 00:13:08.163
no, he's awesome.

00:13:08.163 --> 00:13:08.996
- He's a great actor.

00:13:08.996 --> 00:13:10.441
He's a very dignified,

00:13:10.441 --> 00:13:12.532
very talented African American actor.

00:13:12.532 --> 00:13:14.395
We went to Northwestern
University together,

00:13:14.395 --> 00:13:16.093
but I had not seen Harry in like 20 years

00:13:16.093 --> 00:13:18.100
and he was there the night I did it.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.446
And I'm on the dais and I've just finished

00:13:20.446 --> 00:13:21.279
and nobody's looking at me.

00:13:21.279 --> 00:13:22.444
Like no one will make eye contact

00:13:22.444 --> 00:13:23.944
and Harry comes up

00:13:24.888 --> 00:13:26.339
and he's at the dais

00:13:26.339 --> 00:13:27.696
and everybody's still up there

00:13:27.696 --> 00:13:29.513
and he goes, hey man,
that was really good.

00:13:29.513 --> 00:13:32.889
I said, Harry, it's
really great to see you,

00:13:32.889 --> 00:13:35.818
I don't think these people liked it.

00:13:35.818 --> 00:13:38.959
And Harry's so wonderful, so dignified,

00:13:38.959 --> 00:13:40.833
and just got a fantastic voice.

00:13:40.833 --> 00:13:42.750
He leans back and goes,

00:13:43.753 --> 00:13:45.573
fuck these people.

00:13:45.573 --> 00:13:48.269
(audience laughing)

00:13:48.269 --> 00:13:49.291
- He said it there?

00:13:49.291 --> 00:13:50.795
- That was good.
- He said it there.

00:13:50.795 --> 00:13:53.295
- Oh my god, that's fantastic.

00:13:54.574 --> 00:13:57.668
- So Larry, fuck these people.

00:13:57.668 --> 00:13:59.146
- Yes!
- That was good.

00:13:59.146 --> 00:14:00.450
- That's what I'm talking about.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:01.834
- What's interesting about,

00:14:01.834 --> 00:14:04.125
I think there is union in your character

00:14:04.125 --> 00:14:05.141
and you personally.

00:14:05.141 --> 00:14:06.936
You're very supportive of the troops

00:14:06.936 --> 00:14:10.590
and I remember when you
were in Iraq, right?

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:13.643
You got the president to
order you to get a haircut.

00:14:13.643 --> 00:14:14.476
Pretty serious?

00:14:14.476 --> 00:14:15.651
- That was fun, yeah, sure, yeah.

00:14:15.651 --> 00:14:18.636
I knew I wanted to have my head shaved,

00:14:18.636 --> 00:14:21.861
'cause I thought that'd
feel good in the room.

00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:23.826
That'll feel good with all the troops

00:14:23.826 --> 00:14:25.331
and I said, who could shave my head?

00:14:25.331 --> 00:14:26.634
The General.

00:14:26.634 --> 00:14:27.816
Who's going to make the General do it?

00:14:27.816 --> 00:14:30.599
The president and everybody said yes,

00:14:30.599 --> 00:14:31.633
it was really lovely.

00:14:31.633 --> 00:14:33.688
- And I think this work you're doing

00:14:33.688 --> 00:14:35.195
with the troops is fantastic.

00:14:35.195 --> 00:14:36.749
Was there some reason,

00:14:36.749 --> 00:14:38.442
in sort of growing up or something,

00:14:38.442 --> 00:14:39.275
that you felt that way

00:14:39.275 --> 00:14:42.982
or was it just a genuine
patriot about this stuff?

00:14:42.982 --> 00:14:44.577
- I don't know, I'm a genuine patriot,

00:14:44.577 --> 00:14:46.303
I love my country

00:14:46.303 --> 00:14:50.333
and I think patriotism does not
require focus on the troops.

00:14:50.333 --> 00:14:51.514
Do you know what I mean?

00:14:51.514 --> 00:14:52.808
There are other ways to be patriotic,

00:14:52.808 --> 00:14:56.075
other than association with the military.

00:14:56.075 --> 00:14:59.356
That being said, I think
not enough attention is paid

00:14:59.356 --> 00:15:01.716
to the men and women
who make the sacrifices

00:15:01.716 --> 00:15:04.229
that we have collectively
decided they should make

00:15:04.229 --> 00:15:05.725
and then ignore.
- Yes, I agree.

00:15:05.725 --> 00:15:07.115
- You know what I mean?

00:15:07.115 --> 00:15:08.445
We're all responsible,

00:15:08.445 --> 00:15:12.333
we all are sending the troops
orders, you know what I mean?

00:15:12.333 --> 00:15:14.318
And we did it without a lot of thought

00:15:14.318 --> 00:15:15.674
but with a lot of emotion,

00:15:15.674 --> 00:15:17.091
10, 11 years ago.

00:15:18.522 --> 00:15:20.032
And not a lot of discussion,

00:15:20.032 --> 00:15:22.637
and then we thought our job was done.

00:15:22.637 --> 00:15:24.622
And so, because I talk about it a lot,

00:15:24.622 --> 00:15:26.010
or used to talk about it more,

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:27.259
when it was more in the consciousness,

00:15:27.259 --> 00:15:28.587
especially in the news consciousness

00:15:28.587 --> 00:15:30.763
because my show was a
shadow of the actual news

00:15:30.763 --> 00:15:33.239
and I'm, in some ways,
very reactive that way.

00:15:33.239 --> 00:15:36.101
I felt at a certain point

00:15:36.101 --> 00:15:38.801
that I had a responsibility,

00:15:38.801 --> 00:15:42.227
along with my responsibility to be funny,

00:15:42.227 --> 00:15:45.715
to take opportunities that came to me,

00:15:45.715 --> 00:15:47.253
to talk about the troops when I can.

00:15:47.253 --> 00:15:50.753
I have an '82nd airborne flag in my office

00:15:52.649 --> 00:15:54.208
because very early on in the show,

00:15:54.208 --> 00:15:55.951
a young man and his wife came

00:15:55.951 --> 00:15:57.501
and she had to speak for him

00:15:57.501 --> 00:15:59.631
because he had such bad brain damage

00:15:59.631 --> 00:16:02.243
and he still could hear,

00:16:02.243 --> 00:16:03.656
but he couldn't really converse

00:16:03.656 --> 00:16:05.176
and he enjoyed the show

00:16:05.176 --> 00:16:06.614
and he gave me the flag

00:16:06.614 --> 00:16:07.756
and all he could really get out was,

00:16:07.756 --> 00:16:09.072
don't forget us.

00:16:09.072 --> 00:16:10.810
Please keep talking about us.

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.213
So I've got it on my wall

00:16:12.213 --> 00:16:13.334
and I think about it

00:16:13.334 --> 00:16:14.463
and we don't nearly do enough

00:16:14.463 --> 00:16:16.883
and we don't help as much as we should.

00:16:16.883 --> 00:16:19.312
But certainly, when
you have an opportunity

00:16:19.312 --> 00:16:21.174
that fits within,

00:16:21.174 --> 00:16:23.296
I still have a responsibility
to do a comedy show.

00:16:23.296 --> 00:16:25.032
When I can fit those two things together,

00:16:25.032 --> 00:16:28.238
we're more than happy to
try and make it happen

00:16:28.238 --> 00:16:30.689
and as I said, we should do more.

00:16:30.689 --> 00:16:32.280
When I came back to The Daily Show,

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:34.117
Jon was the new host

00:16:34.117 --> 00:16:36.282
and it was a totally different show,

00:16:36.282 --> 00:16:39.126
'cause Jon invited all of
us who were on the show.

00:16:39.126 --> 00:16:43.293
Doing good jokes is a virtue
in of itself, but he said,

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:46.387
have your own opinion.
- Right.

00:16:46.387 --> 00:16:48.505
- Bring your own passion to the show.

00:16:48.505 --> 00:16:50.836
I want everybody to invest their own ideas

00:16:50.836 --> 00:16:54.467
and I didn't realize I
had political opinions.

00:16:54.467 --> 00:16:55.561
I didn't realize--

00:16:55.561 --> 00:16:57.049
- 'cause were you political before then?

00:16:57.049 --> 00:16:58.766
- No, no, I was always a news junkie,

00:16:58.766 --> 00:17:02.423
but I worked very hard not
to do any political humor

00:17:02.423 --> 00:17:04.590
because there wasn't a lot

00:17:05.615 --> 00:17:06.989
that I enjoyed.
- Yeah.

00:17:06.989 --> 00:17:08.362
- I like George Carlin,

00:17:08.362 --> 00:17:10.450
which was certainly satire.
- Yeah.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:13.622
- And social satire, not
a lot of political satire

00:17:13.622 --> 00:17:15.894
and so I really admired George Carlin

00:17:15.894 --> 00:17:17.221
and kind of wanted to be him

00:17:17.221 --> 00:17:19.041
or be him or Steve Martin.

00:17:19.041 --> 00:17:21.392
But didn't do political things.

00:17:21.392 --> 00:17:23.745
I made a very specific
choice in all my work

00:17:23.745 --> 00:17:26.356
to not even reference real
world events or real places

00:17:26.356 --> 00:17:28.017
or who the president was.

00:17:28.017 --> 00:17:29.034
- Why?

00:17:29.034 --> 00:17:32.603
- Because I wanted people to
enjoy my joke a year from now,

00:17:32.603 --> 00:17:34.511
as opposed to right now.
- And you didn't think

00:17:34.511 --> 00:17:37.561
that current would be
relevant a year from now?

00:17:37.561 --> 00:17:39.261
- Well, there also wasn't
a lot of it I liked.

00:17:39.261 --> 00:17:40.293
I thought there was a lot of too many

00:17:40.293 --> 00:17:41.373
Ted Kennedy drunk jokes.

00:17:41.373 --> 00:17:42.902
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Do you know what I mean?

00:17:42.902 --> 00:17:44.399
Which is very flippant.
- Yeah.

00:17:44.399 --> 00:17:47.335
- But isn't really, there's
not a lot of intellectual sweat

00:17:47.335 --> 00:17:48.459
that goes into that joke,

00:17:48.459 --> 00:17:50.123
so I wanted to do something
just different than that.

00:17:50.123 --> 00:17:52.027
But, Jon--
- I like that term,

00:17:52.027 --> 00:17:53.325
intellectual sweat.

00:17:53.325 --> 00:17:54.938
- Yeah--
- Because that really is

00:17:54.938 --> 00:17:56.150
what your work is.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:58.084
It's kind of intellectually sweaty.

00:17:58.084 --> 00:17:59.951
- Yeah, well we're working really hard

00:17:59.951 --> 00:18:01.869
to make jokes about ideas.

00:18:01.869 --> 00:18:03.805
- Speaking of which, again,

00:18:03.805 --> 00:18:05.613
many Googlers have been
asking questions about

00:18:05.613 --> 00:18:07.863
your upcoming YouTube show.

00:18:09.058 --> 00:18:10.630
- Yeah, what is that?

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:12.231
(audience laughing)

00:18:12.231 --> 00:18:13.600
I have an upcoming YouTube show?

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:14.539
- Yes, we have all decided

00:18:14.539 --> 00:18:15.698
that you have to have a YouTube show,

00:18:15.698 --> 00:18:17.710
of some kind.
- Okay.

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:20.936
Does that violate my contract
with Viacom to have that?

00:18:20.936 --> 00:18:22.361
'cause you guys had a billion dollar

00:18:22.361 --> 00:18:24.918
lawsuit against each other, you realize?

00:18:24.918 --> 00:18:26.194
And Sumner Redstone would rather

00:18:26.194 --> 00:18:27.027
see your head on a stick.

00:18:27.027 --> 00:18:29.120
- You actually asked us on television,

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:30.847
to give you the money, you forget?

00:18:30.847 --> 00:18:31.991
- No, I don't forget.

00:18:31.991 --> 00:18:33.576
You never gave it to me.
(audience laughing)

00:18:33.576 --> 00:18:35.005
- I know, I know.

00:18:35.005 --> 00:18:36.460
- If you gave the money,

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:37.293
I'd be knee deep,
- we discussed this

00:18:37.293 --> 00:18:38.359
- In hookers and blow and I'd forget.

00:18:38.359 --> 00:18:40.094
(audience laughing)

00:18:40.094 --> 00:18:41.601
No, no, I was deposed.

00:18:41.601 --> 00:18:43.087
I was deposed for that lawsuit.

00:18:43.087 --> 00:18:44.062
- I know you were.

00:18:44.062 --> 00:18:46.027
- And I've got a good story
if you'd stop talking.

00:18:46.027 --> 00:18:49.317
(audience laughing)

00:18:49.317 --> 00:18:50.322
I'm sorry, what was your question?

00:18:50.322 --> 00:18:52.133
- Tell your story.

00:18:52.133 --> 00:18:54.422
- Okay, okay.
(audience laughing)

00:18:54.422 --> 00:18:55.441
So, it's been a few years

00:18:55.441 --> 00:18:56.444
and I don't think I'm violating it,

00:18:56.444 --> 00:18:59.548
but I got deposed for
the YouTube Google thing,

00:18:59.548 --> 00:19:00.696
the Viacom thing,

00:19:00.696 --> 00:19:03.236
'cause boy, they were mad at you guys.

00:19:03.236 --> 00:19:04.771
They were so mad at you guys

00:19:04.771 --> 00:19:06.427
and so, your all's lawyer,

00:19:06.427 --> 00:19:07.882
brought me in to say,

00:19:07.882 --> 00:19:09.901
well, how isn't YouTube great

00:19:09.901 --> 00:19:11.731
and wouldn't you not have a show,

00:19:11.731 --> 00:19:12.564
if it wasn't for YouTube?

00:19:12.564 --> 00:19:13.397
And all those kind of questions

00:19:13.397 --> 00:19:15.480
and the lawyer for Google

00:19:17.028 --> 00:19:19.230
would read these statements
that I said on air

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:21.137
and I said, well, I didn't say that.

00:19:21.137 --> 00:19:23.850
And he goes, no, you said it,

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:24.949
you said it on this broadcast.

00:19:24.949 --> 00:19:26.497
I said, no, my character said that

00:19:26.497 --> 00:19:29.183
and my characters not
under oath right now.

00:19:29.183 --> 00:19:31.042
(audience laughing)

00:19:31.042 --> 00:19:34.334
and they said, if your
character were here,

00:19:34.334 --> 00:19:37.334
(audience laughing)

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:41.210
what would he say?

00:19:43.278 --> 00:19:44.802
And so, I would say,

00:19:44.802 --> 00:19:48.087
okay, well, I'll tell you
what, I'll tell you what.

00:19:48.087 --> 00:19:51.256
If you ask me questions that
my character has to answer,

00:19:51.256 --> 00:19:52.258
as opposed to me,

00:19:52.258 --> 00:19:56.920
I'll move my coffee cup to the
other side of my place here

00:19:56.920 --> 00:19:59.120
and then you'll know I'm
speaking as my character.

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:00.596
And so, they'd ask me a question

00:20:00.596 --> 00:20:01.875
and I'd keep my cup over here.

00:20:01.875 --> 00:20:03.573
And then in the middle of the question,

00:20:03.573 --> 00:20:05.593
I'd start moving it over to the side,

00:20:05.593 --> 00:20:07.414
'cause I'd realize they
were asking me something

00:20:07.414 --> 00:20:09.152
from my character and the person would go,

00:20:09.152 --> 00:20:10.980
let the record reflect,

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:14.231
the coffee cup is now on the
left side of Mr. Colbert.

00:20:14.231 --> 00:20:17.678
(audience applause)

00:20:17.678 --> 00:20:19.511
- You have a question?

00:20:20.782 --> 00:20:22.070
- My characters answer was always like,

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:23.293
I don't know what you're talking about,

00:20:23.293 --> 00:20:27.756
I don't know what you're
talking about, buddy.

00:20:27.756 --> 00:20:28.837
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:20:28.837 --> 00:20:30.036
I hoped you enjoyed.

00:20:30.036 --> 00:20:31.400
I made this video because Adam Jyun,

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:34.257
I hoped a pronounced
that okay, asked me to.

00:20:34.257 --> 00:20:35.348
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:20:35.348 --> 00:20:36.981
that you want me to profile next,

00:20:36.981 --> 00:20:38.190
leave it down in the comments below

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.380
and I'll see what I can do.

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:40.847
I'd also love to know which of the clips

00:20:40.847 --> 00:20:42.078
had the biggest impact on you.

00:20:42.078 --> 00:20:43.963
What are you going to change
in your business or life?

00:20:43.963 --> 00:20:45.317
How are you going to apply

00:20:45.317 --> 00:20:47.500
some of the advice
Stephen had to yourself?

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:49.040
Leave it in the comments

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:50.461
and I'm super curious,

00:20:50.461 --> 00:20:51.725
'cause I want to join in the discussion.

00:20:51.725 --> 00:20:52.982
I also want to give a
quick shout out the Ola,

00:20:52.982 --> 00:20:55.337
from empiretrafficacademy.com.

00:20:55.337 --> 00:20:57.762
Ola, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book.

00:20:57.762 --> 00:21:00.007
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:21:00.007 --> 00:21:01.122
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:21:01.122 --> 00:21:02.397
I believe in you,

00:21:02.397 --> 00:21:05.073
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:21:05.073 --> 00:21:08.464
and whatever your one
word is, much love guys.

00:21:08.464 --> 00:21:09.964
I'll see you soon.

00:21:16.163 --> 00:21:18.475
- [EmCee] No one is safe,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:21:18.475 --> 00:21:19.592
here with a special edition

00:21:19.592 --> 00:21:23.059
of The Colbert Report, Stephen Colbert.

00:21:23.059 --> 00:21:26.059
(audience applause)

00:21:31.338 --> 00:21:34.083
- Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.

00:21:34.083 --> 00:21:36.550
Before I begin, I've been
asked to make an announcement.

00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:40.717
Who ever parked 14 black
bulletproof SUVs out front,

00:21:42.284 --> 00:21:43.788
could you please remove them,

00:21:43.788 --> 00:21:48.758
they're blocking in 14
other black bulletproof SUVs

00:21:48.758 --> 00:21:50.258
and they need out.

00:21:52.644 --> 00:21:54.644
Wow, wow, what an honor.

00:21:55.819 --> 00:21:57.773
The White House Correspondents Dinner.

00:21:57.773 --> 00:22:00.356
To sit here, at the same table,

00:22:01.723 --> 00:22:04.140
with my hero, George W. Bush.

00:22:05.118 --> 00:22:07.596
To be this close to the man.

00:22:07.596 --> 00:22:10.548
I feel like I'm dreaming,
somebody pinch me.

00:22:10.548 --> 00:22:11.849
You know what?

00:22:11.849 --> 00:22:13.199
I'm a pretty sound sleeper,

00:22:13.199 --> 00:22:14.032
that may not be enough.

00:22:14.032 --> 00:22:15.958
Somebody shoot me in the face.

00:22:15.958 --> 00:22:17.254
(audience laughing)

00:22:17.254 --> 00:22:20.040
Is he really not here tonight?

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:23.373
Damnit, the one guy who could've helped.

00:22:24.651 --> 00:22:25.938
By the way, before I get started,

00:22:25.938 --> 00:22:27.773
anybody needs anything
else at their tables,

00:22:27.773 --> 00:22:30.898
just speak slowly and clearly
into your table numbers,

00:22:30.898 --> 00:22:35.065
someone from the NSA will be
right over with a cocktail.

00:22:38.606 --> 00:22:42.297
Mark Smith, ladies and
gentlemen of the press corp,

00:22:42.297 --> 00:22:44.869
Madam First Lady, Mr. President,

00:22:44.869 --> 00:22:47.357
my name is Stephen Colbert

00:22:47.357 --> 00:22:49.069
and tonight it is my privilege

00:22:49.069 --> 00:22:51.402
to celebrate this president.

00:22:52.953 --> 00:22:56.380
'cause, we're not so different, he and I.

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:57.630
We both get it.

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:02.356
Guys like us, we're not some
brainiacs on the nerd patrol,

00:23:02.356 --> 00:23:05.356
we're not members of the factinista.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.616
We go straight from the gut, right sir?

00:23:09.616 --> 00:23:10.616
That's where the truth lies,

00:23:10.616 --> 00:23:12.866
right down here in the gut.

00:23:14.626 --> 00:23:16.337
Do you know you have more
nerve endings in your gut,

00:23:16.337 --> 00:23:19.119
than you have in your head?

00:23:19.119 --> 00:23:20.227
(audience laughing)

00:23:20.227 --> 00:23:21.342
You can look it up.

00:23:21.342 --> 00:23:24.398
Now, I know some of you are
going to say I did look it up

00:23:24.398 --> 00:23:25.231
and that's not true.

00:23:25.231 --> 00:23:28.460
(audience laughing)

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:31.651
That's 'cause you looked it up in a book.

00:23:31.651 --> 00:23:35.068
Next time, look it up in your gut, I did.

00:23:35.924 --> 00:23:39.602
My gut tells me that's how
our nervous system works.

00:23:39.602 --> 00:23:43.082
Every night, on my show,
the Colbert Report,

00:23:43.082 --> 00:23:45.732
I speak straight from the gut, okay?

00:23:45.732 --> 00:23:49.899
I give people the truth
unfiltered by rational argument.

00:23:52.897 --> 00:23:55.638
I call it the no fact zone.

00:23:55.638 --> 00:23:58.858
FOX news, I want a copyright on that term.

00:23:58.858 --> 00:24:01.858
(audience laughing)

00:24:04.397 --> 00:24:05.947
I'm a simple man with a simple mind.

00:24:05.947 --> 00:24:10.238
I hold a simple set of
beliefs that I live by.

00:24:10.238 --> 00:24:13.782
Number one, I believe in America.

00:24:13.782 --> 00:24:15.449
I believe it exists.

00:24:17.772 --> 00:24:20.901
My gut tells me I live there.

00:24:20.901 --> 00:24:24.565
I feel that it extends from
the Atlantic to the Pacific.

00:24:24.565 --> 00:24:28.053
And I strongly believe it has 50 states

00:24:28.053 --> 00:24:30.234
and I cannot wait to see
how the Washington Post

00:24:30.234 --> 00:24:32.234
spins that one tomorrow.

00:24:34.088 --> 00:24:36.258
I believe in democracy.

00:24:36.258 --> 00:24:39.826
I believe democracy is
our greatest export,

00:24:39.826 --> 00:24:41.829
at least until China figures out a way

00:24:41.829 --> 00:24:45.321
to stamp it out of
plastic for $0.03 a unit.

00:24:45.321 --> 00:24:46.808
As a matter of fact,

00:24:46.808 --> 00:24:49.541
Ambassador Zhou Wenzhong, welcome.

00:24:49.541 --> 00:24:52.711
Your great country makes
our happy meals possible.

00:24:52.711 --> 00:24:55.711
(audience laughing)

00:24:58.577 --> 00:25:00.660
I said it to celebration.

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:05.462
I believe the government
that governs best,

00:25:05.462 --> 00:25:07.340
is the government that governs least

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:08.535
and by these standards,

00:25:08.535 --> 00:25:12.620
we have setup a fabulous
government in Iraq.

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:15.620
(audience laughing)

00:25:17.885 --> 00:25:21.975
I believe in pulling yourself
up by your own boot straps.

00:25:21.975 --> 00:25:23.200
I believe it is possible.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:25.509
I saw this guy do it
once in Cirque du Soleil.

00:25:25.509 --> 00:25:26.947
(audience laughing)

00:25:26.947 --> 00:25:28.197
It was magical.

00:25:32.202 --> 00:25:33.652
And though I am a committed Christian,

00:25:33.652 --> 00:25:37.856
I believe that everyone has the
right to their own religion,

00:25:37.856 --> 00:25:40.088
be you Hindu, Jewish, or Muslim,

00:25:40.088 --> 00:25:41.630
I believe there are infinite paths

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:44.556
to accepting Jesus Christ
as your personal Savior.

00:25:44.556 --> 00:25:47.556
(audience laughing)

00:25:49.881 --> 00:25:53.548
Ladies and gentlemen,
I believe it's yogurt.

00:25:58.429 --> 00:26:01.762
But I refuse to believe it's not butter.

00:26:05.448 --> 00:26:07.936
Most of all, I believe in this president.

00:26:07.936 --> 00:26:09.955
Now I know there are some
polls out there saying

00:26:09.955 --> 00:26:12.805
that this man has a 32% approval rating.

00:26:12.805 --> 00:26:15.968
But guys like us, we don't
pay attention to the polls.

00:26:15.968 --> 00:26:20.072
We know that polls are just
a collection of statistics

00:26:20.072 --> 00:26:24.971
that reflect what people
are thinking in reality.

00:26:24.971 --> 00:26:28.471
And reality has a well known liberal bias.

00:26:30.472 --> 00:26:31.885
So Mr. President, please pay no attention

00:26:31.885 --> 00:26:34.814
to the people who say
the glass is half full.

00:26:34.814 --> 00:26:38.981
32% means that glass (laughs),

00:26:41.233 --> 00:26:44.716
it's important to set up
your jokes properly, Sir.

00:26:44.716 --> 00:26:46.316
Sir, pay no attention
to the people who say

00:26:46.316 --> 00:26:48.200
the glass is half empty.

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:51.950
Because 32% means it's two thirds empty.

00:26:53.308 --> 00:26:57.833
There's still some liquid
in that glass, is my point.

00:26:57.833 --> 00:26:59.379
But I wouldn't drink it.

00:26:59.379 --> 00:27:00.662
(audience laughing)

00:27:00.662 --> 00:27:03.329
Last third is usually back wash.

00:27:07.061 --> 00:27:09.061
Look folks, my point is,

00:27:10.008 --> 00:27:13.063
that I don't believe this is a
low point in this presidency.

00:27:13.063 --> 00:27:15.403
I believe it is just a lull,

00:27:15.403 --> 00:27:17.720
before a comeback.

00:27:17.720 --> 00:27:19.995
I mean, it's like the
movie, Rocky, alright?

00:27:19.995 --> 00:27:22.384
The president, in this
case, is Rocky Balboa,

00:27:22.384 --> 00:27:26.467
and Apollo Creed is
everything else in the world.

00:27:27.744 --> 00:27:29.005
(audience laughing)

00:27:29.005 --> 00:27:31.736
So 10th round, he's bloodied.

00:27:31.736 --> 00:27:34.113
His corner man, Mick, who
in this case, I guess,

00:27:34.113 --> 00:27:35.162
would be the vice president.

00:27:35.162 --> 00:27:37.436
He's yelling, cut me Dick, cut me.

00:27:37.436 --> 00:27:39.171
And every time he falls,

00:27:39.171 --> 00:27:41.889
everyone says, stay down Rock, stay down.

00:27:41.889 --> 00:27:43.178
But does he stay down?

00:27:43.178 --> 00:27:45.876
No, like Rocky, he gets back up

00:27:45.876 --> 00:27:50.043
and in the end he actually
loses in the first movie.

00:27:53.159 --> 00:27:54.421
Okay, doesn't matter.

00:27:54.421 --> 00:27:56.800
Doesn't matter, the point is,

00:27:56.800 --> 00:27:58.508
it is the heart warming story

00:27:58.508 --> 00:28:01.909
of a man who is repeatedly
punched in the face.

00:28:01.909 --> 00:28:04.909
(audience laughing)

00:28:07.221 --> 00:28:09.388
So don't pay attention
to the approval ratings

00:28:09.388 --> 00:28:12.997
that say it's 68% of Americans disapprove

00:28:12.997 --> 00:28:15.759
of the job this man is doing.

00:28:15.759 --> 00:28:16.772
I ask you this,

00:28:16.772 --> 00:28:18.131
does that not also logically mean

00:28:18.131 --> 00:28:22.298
that 68% of approve of
the job he's not doing?

00:28:25.534 --> 00:28:27.701
Think about it, I haven't.

00:28:33.182 --> 00:28:35.317
I stand by this man.

00:28:35.317 --> 00:28:38.444
I stand by this man because
he stands for things.

00:28:38.444 --> 00:28:42.036
Not only for things, he stands on things.

00:28:42.036 --> 00:28:44.470
Things like aircraft carriers and rubble

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:47.777
and recently flooded city squares.

00:28:47.777 --> 00:28:49.647
And that sends a strong message,

00:28:49.647 --> 00:28:52.927
that no matter what happens to America,

00:28:52.927 --> 00:28:55.843
she will always rebound with
the most powerfully staged

00:28:55.843 --> 00:28:57.760
photo-ops in the world.

00:29:00.737 --> 00:29:02.196
Now, there may be an energy crisis.

00:29:02.196 --> 00:29:03.777
Well this president has a

00:29:03.777 --> 00:29:06.637
very forward thinking energy policy.

00:29:06.637 --> 00:29:08.800
Why do you think he's down on the ranch

00:29:08.800 --> 00:29:11.693
cutting that brush all the time?

00:29:11.693 --> 00:29:15.384
He's trying to create an
alternative energy source.

00:29:15.384 --> 00:29:19.134
By 2008, we will have
a mesquite powered car.

00:29:22.436 --> 00:29:24.350
And I just like the guy.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:25.608
He's a good Joe.

00:29:25.608 --> 00:29:28.053
Obviously loves his wife,

00:29:28.053 --> 00:29:30.026
calls her his better half

00:29:30.026 --> 00:29:32.387
and polls show, America agrees.

00:29:32.387 --> 00:29:34.775
(audience laughing)

00:29:34.775 --> 00:29:36.970
She's a true lady and a wonderful woman,

00:29:36.970 --> 00:29:38.684
but I just have one beef, ma'am.

00:29:38.684 --> 00:29:41.407
I'm sorry, but this reading initiative.

00:29:41.407 --> 00:29:44.494
I'm sorry, I've never been a fan of books.

00:29:44.494 --> 00:29:45.940
I don't trust them,

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:48.190
they're all fact, no heart.

00:29:49.071 --> 00:29:52.433
I mean, they're elitists
telling us what is or isn't true

00:29:52.433 --> 00:29:53.876
or did or didn't happen.

00:29:53.876 --> 00:29:55.851
Who's Britanica to tell me

00:29:55.851 --> 00:29:58.851
that Panama Canal was built in 1914?

00:30:00.276 --> 00:30:02.385
If I want to say it was built in 1941,

00:30:02.385 --> 00:30:04.903
that's my right as an American.

00:30:04.903 --> 00:30:06.428
I'm with the president, let history decide

00:30:06.428 --> 00:30:08.678
what did or did not happen.

00:30:12.024 --> 00:30:13.742
The greatest thing about this man,

00:30:13.742 --> 00:30:15.938
is he's steady, you know where he stands.

00:30:15.938 --> 00:30:18.656
He believes the same thing Wednesday,

00:30:18.656 --> 00:30:21.279
that he believed on Monday.

00:30:21.279 --> 00:30:24.621
No matter what happened Tuesday.

00:30:24.621 --> 00:30:28.704
Events can change, this
man's beliefs never will.

00:30:32.295 --> 00:30:36.378
As excited as I am to be
here with the president,

00:30:37.264 --> 00:30:40.505
I am appalled to be surrounded
by the liberal media

00:30:40.505 --> 00:30:43.170
that is destroying America.

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:46.539
With the exception of FOX news.

00:30:46.539 --> 00:30:49.637
Fox news gives you both
sides of every story.

00:30:49.637 --> 00:30:53.804
The president's side and
the vice president's side.

00:30:56.377 --> 00:30:57.819
But the rest of you,
what are you thinking?

00:30:57.819 --> 00:30:59.848
Reporting on NSA wiretapping

00:30:59.848 --> 00:31:02.677
or secret prisons in Eastern Europe?

00:31:02.677 --> 00:31:07.430
Those things are secret for
a very important reason.

00:31:07.430 --> 00:31:09.513
They're super depressing.

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:15.017
And if that's your goal,
well, misery accomplished.

00:31:15.017 --> 00:31:16.083
Over the last five years,

00:31:16.083 --> 00:31:18.738
you people were so good.

00:31:18.738 --> 00:31:21.405
Over tax cuts, WMD intelligence,

00:31:23.074 --> 00:31:24.516
the effect of global warming.

00:31:24.516 --> 00:31:26.336
We Americans didn't want to know

00:31:26.336 --> 00:31:30.697
and you had the courtesy
not to try to find out.

00:31:30.697 --> 00:31:32.610
Those were good times,

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:34.110
as far as we knew.

00:31:36.051 --> 00:31:37.570
But listen, let's review the rules.

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:38.947
Here's how it works.

00:31:38.947 --> 00:31:41.310
The president makes decisions,

00:31:41.310 --> 00:31:42.727
he's the decider.

00:31:43.660 --> 00:31:46.515
The press secretary
announces those decisions

00:31:46.515 --> 00:31:47.946
and you people of the press,

00:31:47.946 --> 00:31:49.602
type those decisions down.

00:31:49.602 --> 00:31:51.755
Make, announce, type.

00:31:51.755 --> 00:31:55.006
Just put them through a
spellcheck and go home.

00:31:55.006 --> 00:31:56.350
Get to know your family again,

00:31:56.350 --> 00:31:59.074
make love to your wife.

00:31:59.074 --> 00:32:00.918
Write that novel you got
kicking around in your head.

00:32:00.918 --> 00:32:05.216
You know, the one about the
intrepid Washington reporter

00:32:05.216 --> 00:32:07.964
with the courage to stand
up to the administration?

00:32:07.964 --> 00:32:10.015
You know, fiction!

00:32:10.015 --> 00:32:13.015
(audience laughing)

00:32:16.044 --> 00:32:19.461
'cause really, what incentive
do these people have

00:32:19.461 --> 00:32:22.257
to answer your questions after all?

00:32:22.257 --> 00:32:24.565
I mean, nothing satisfies you.

00:32:24.565 --> 00:32:27.084
Everybody asks for personnel changes.

00:32:27.084 --> 00:32:29.327
So, the White House has personnel changes

00:32:29.327 --> 00:32:30.692
and then you write,

00:32:30.692 --> 00:32:35.333
oh they're just rearranging
the deck chairs on the Titanic.

00:32:35.333 --> 00:32:38.759
First of all, that is a terrible metaphor.

00:32:38.759 --> 00:32:41.234
This administration is not sinking.

00:32:41.234 --> 00:32:44.267
This administration is soaring.

00:32:44.267 --> 00:32:46.271
If anything, they are rearranging

00:32:46.271 --> 00:32:48.585
the deck chairs on the Hindenburg.

00:32:48.585 --> 00:32:51.585
(audience laughing)

00:32:57.132 --> 00:32:59.130
now it's not all bad guys out there.

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:00.553
There's some of the
heroes out there tonight.

00:33:00.553 --> 00:33:03.695
Christopher Buckley, Jeff
Saks, Ken Burns, Bob Shieffer.

00:33:03.695 --> 00:33:05.346
I've interviewed all of them.

00:33:05.346 --> 00:33:06.179
By the way, Mr. President,

00:33:06.179 --> 00:33:07.479
thank you for agreeing to be on my show.

00:33:07.479 --> 00:33:10.939
I appreciate it, I was just as shocked

00:33:10.939 --> 00:33:14.161
as everyone here is, I promise you.

00:33:14.161 --> 00:33:15.994
How's Tuesday for you?

00:33:17.088 --> 00:33:19.995
I've got Frank Rich, but
we can just bump him.

00:33:19.995 --> 00:33:21.750
And I mean bump him.

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:22.748
(audience laughing)

00:33:22.748 --> 00:33:24.998
I know a guy, say the word.

00:33:30.559 --> 00:33:32.422
See who we've got here tonight.

00:33:32.422 --> 00:33:35.097
We've got General Moseley,
Air Force Chief of Staff.

00:33:35.097 --> 00:33:36.482
We've got General Peter Pace,

00:33:36.482 --> 00:33:37.513
Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.

00:33:37.513 --> 00:33:39.181
They still support Rumsfeld.

00:33:39.181 --> 00:33:43.360
Right, you guys aren't retired yet, right?

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:46.277
Right, they still support Rumsfeld.

00:33:47.871 --> 00:33:50.031
Look, by the way, I've got a theory

00:33:50.031 --> 00:33:52.039
about how to handle these retired generals

00:33:52.039 --> 00:33:53.729
causing all this trouble:

00:33:53.729 --> 00:33:55.736
Don't let them retire!

00:33:55.736 --> 00:33:58.588
Come on, we've got a stop-loss program;

00:33:58.588 --> 00:34:00.953
let's use it on these guys.

00:34:00.953 --> 00:34:03.139
I've seen Zinni in that
crowd on Wolf Blitzer.

00:34:03.139 --> 00:34:05.341
If you're strong enough to go
on one of those pundit shows,

00:34:05.341 --> 00:34:07.708
you're strong enough to
stand on a bank of computers

00:34:07.708 --> 00:34:10.625
and order men into battle, come on!

00:34:16.813 --> 00:34:19.318
Jesse Jackson is here, the Reverend.

00:34:19.318 --> 00:34:20.911
Haven't heard from the Reverend
in just a little while.

00:34:20.911 --> 00:34:22.411
I had him on the show.

00:34:22.411 --> 00:34:23.566
It was a very interesting interview,

00:34:23.566 --> 00:34:25.154
very challenging interview.

00:34:25.154 --> 00:34:26.583
You can ask him anything,

00:34:26.583 --> 00:34:29.154
but he's going to say what he wants

00:34:29.154 --> 00:34:31.321
at the pace that he wants.

00:34:32.462 --> 00:34:35.363
It's like boxing a glacier.

00:34:35.363 --> 00:34:36.928
Enjoy that metaphor, by the way,

00:34:36.928 --> 00:34:38.736
because your grandchildren
will have no idea

00:34:38.736 --> 00:34:39.569
what a glacier is.

00:34:39.569 --> 00:34:42.569
(audience laughing)

00:34:46.149 --> 00:34:48.629
Justice Scalia is here.

00:34:48.629 --> 00:34:53.479
Justice Scalia, may I be the
first to say, welcome, sir.

00:34:53.479 --> 00:34:56.146
You look fantastic, how are you?

00:35:04.979 --> 00:35:08.479
Just talking some Sicilian with my paisan.

00:35:14.225 --> 00:35:16.483
John McCain is here.

00:35:16.483 --> 00:35:18.709
John McCain, what a maverick!

00:35:18.709 --> 00:35:22.289
Somebody find out what
fork he used on his salad,

00:35:22.289 --> 00:35:25.423
because I guarantee you
it wasn't a salad fork.

00:35:25.423 --> 00:35:26.820
This guy could have used a spoon!

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:28.987
There's no predicting him.

00:35:30.061 --> 00:35:31.179
By the way, Senator McCain,

00:35:31.179 --> 00:35:32.639
it's so wonderful to see you coming back

00:35:32.639 --> 00:35:33.976
into the Republican fold.

00:35:33.976 --> 00:35:35.612
I've actually got a summer
house in South Carolina.

00:35:35.612 --> 00:35:38.436
Look me up when you go to
speak at Bob Jones University.

00:35:38.436 --> 00:35:41.353
So glad you've seen the light, sir.

00:35:43.688 --> 00:35:46.579
Mayor Nagin, Mayor Nagin
is here from New Orleans,

00:35:46.579 --> 00:35:48.162
the chocolate city!

00:35:49.209 --> 00:35:50.626
Yeah, give it up.

00:35:51.594 --> 00:35:54.878
Mayor Nagin, I'd like to
welcome you to Washington, D.C.,

00:35:54.878 --> 00:35:58.132
the chocolate city with
a marshmallow center

00:35:58.132 --> 00:36:01.132
(audience laughing)

00:36:06.338 --> 00:36:09.799
and a graham cracker crust of corruption.

00:36:09.799 --> 00:36:11.408
It's a Mallomar, I guess,
is what I'm describing,

00:36:11.408 --> 00:36:14.575
is a Mallomar, it's a seasonal cookie.

00:36:16.139 --> 00:36:17.501
Joe Wilson is here.

00:36:17.501 --> 00:36:19.526
Joe Wilson, right down here in front,

00:36:19.526 --> 00:36:22.943
the most famous husband since Desi Arnaz.

00:36:26.719 --> 00:36:27.936
And, of course, he brought along

00:36:27.936 --> 00:36:29.544
his lovely wife Valerie Plame.

00:36:29.544 --> 00:36:31.096
Oh, my god!

00:36:31.096 --> 00:36:33.967
(audience laughing)

00:36:33.967 --> 00:36:35.841
Oh, what have I said?

00:36:35.841 --> 00:36:39.089
Ay, gee monetti, I am
sorry, Mr. President,

00:36:39.089 --> 00:36:41.590
I meant to say he brought
along his lovely wife,

00:36:41.590 --> 00:36:43.257
"Joe Wilson's wife."

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:47.910
Patrick Fitzgerald is
not here tonight, right?

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:50.473
Okay, dodged a bullet.

00:36:50.473 --> 00:36:53.081
And, of course, we can't
forget the man of the hour,

00:36:53.081 --> 00:36:55.664
new press secretary, Tony Snow.

00:36:57.709 --> 00:37:00.376
Secret Service name: "Snow Job."

00:37:02.703 --> 00:37:04.200
Toughest job, what a hero!

00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:07.580
Took the second toughest
job in government,

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:11.163
next to, of course,
the ambassador to Iraq.

00:37:12.076 --> 00:37:14.441
Got some big shoes to fill, Tony.

00:37:14.441 --> 00:37:15.966
Big shoes to fill.

00:37:15.966 --> 00:37:20.133
Scott McClellan could say
nothing like nobody else.

00:37:22.829 --> 00:37:23.956
McClellan, of course, eager to retire,

00:37:23.956 --> 00:37:27.179
really felt like he needed to

00:37:27.179 --> 00:37:30.846
spend more time with
Andrew Card's children.

00:37:33.324 --> 00:37:34.634
Now, Mr. President, I wish you hadn't

00:37:34.634 --> 00:37:36.189
made the decision so quickly, sir.

00:37:36.189 --> 00:37:38.637
I was vying for the job myself.

00:37:38.637 --> 00:37:40.977
I think I would have made
a fabulous press secretary.

00:37:40.977 --> 00:37:45.197
I have nothing but
contempt for these people.

00:37:45.197 --> 00:37:47.947
I know how to handle these clowns.

00:37:47.947 --> 00:37:51.379
In fact, sir, I brought
along an audition tape

00:37:51.379 --> 00:37:53.462
and with your indulgence,

00:37:54.331 --> 00:37:57.003
I'd like to at least give it a shot?

00:37:57.003 --> 00:38:00.836
So, ladies and gentleman,
my press conference.

00:38:09.371 --> 00:38:12.702
(camera shutters)

00:38:12.702 --> 00:38:15.515
I have a brief statement.

00:38:15.515 --> 00:38:18.098
The press I destroying America.

00:38:20.283 --> 00:38:22.643
Okay, let's see who we got here today.

00:38:22.643 --> 00:38:24.726
Stretch, Sir Nerdlington.

00:38:27.768 --> 00:38:30.851
Sloppy Joe, Terry Lemon Meringue Pie.

00:38:34.940 --> 00:38:38.684
Oh, Doubting Thomas, always a pleasure.

00:38:38.684 --> 00:38:40.434
And Susanne Malhello.

00:38:47.170 --> 00:38:48.182
Yes?

00:38:48.182 --> 00:38:50.126
- Will the vice president
be available soon

00:38:50.126 --> 00:38:51.836
to answer all questions, himself?

00:38:51.836 --> 00:38:52.844
- I've already addressed
that question, you.

00:38:52.844 --> 00:38:56.721
- Walter Crumbcake, the noted CBS anchor.

00:38:56.721 --> 00:38:59.997
- Ah, no, he's the former CBS anchor.

00:38:59.997 --> 00:39:03.167
Katie Couric is the anchor
the CBS Evening News.

00:39:03.167 --> 00:39:05.207
By the way, how do you
guys feel about that?

00:39:05.207 --> 00:39:07.862
You, toppled hair, guy in the back.

00:39:07.862 --> 00:39:09.055
Are you happy about
Katie Couric taking over

00:39:09.055 --> 00:39:11.051
the CBS Evening News?

00:39:11.051 --> 00:39:13.384
- No, sir, Mr. Colbert, you?

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:18.557
- Oh look, we woke David Gregory up.

00:39:18.557 --> 00:39:19.743
David, do you have a question?

00:39:19.743 --> 00:39:21.636
- Did Karl Robe commit a crime?

00:39:21.636 --> 00:39:22.907
- I don't know, I'll ask him.

00:39:22.907 --> 00:39:25.157
Karl, pay attention please.

00:39:29.099 --> 00:39:32.106
- Do you stand by your
statement from the Fall of 2003,

00:39:32.106 --> 00:39:35.446
when you asked specifically
about Karl and Elliott Abrams,

00:39:35.446 --> 00:39:36.868
and Scooter Livy,

00:39:36.868 --> 00:39:39.134
and you said, I have gone
to each of those gentlemen,

00:39:39.134 --> 00:39:42.089
and they told me they
are not involved in this.

00:39:42.089 --> 00:39:43.380
Do you stand by that statement?

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:44.381
- Nah, I was just kidding.

00:39:44.381 --> 00:39:45.864
- No, you're not finished,
you're not saying anything.

00:39:45.864 --> 00:39:47.475
You stood at that podium--

00:39:47.475 --> 00:39:49.829
- Ah, that's where
you're wrong, new podium.

00:39:49.829 --> 00:39:51.230
Just had it delivered today.

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:52.864
Get your facts straight, David.

00:39:52.864 --> 00:39:54.188
- This is ridiculous,

00:39:54.188 --> 00:39:56.035
the notion that you're
going to stand before us,

00:39:56.035 --> 00:39:58.157
after having commented
with that level of detail

00:39:58.157 --> 00:40:00.253
and tell the people watching this

00:40:00.253 --> 00:40:02.299
and somehow, you've decided not to talk?

00:40:02.299 --> 00:40:04.882
- I'm sorry, what's that David?

00:40:06.681 --> 00:40:09.573
If I can't hear you, I
can't answer your question.

00:40:09.573 --> 00:40:12.620
I'm sorry, I have to move on, yes, Terry.

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:14.514
- After the investigation began,

00:40:14.514 --> 00:40:18.138
after criminal investigation was underway,

00:40:18.138 --> 00:40:21.805
you said (fast forwarding).

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:26.107
All of a sudden, you have
respect for the sanctity

00:40:26.107 --> 00:40:27.392
of the criminal investigation?

00:40:27.392 --> 00:40:29.050
- No, I never had the
respect for the sanctity

00:40:29.050 --> 00:40:31.125
of a criminal investigation.

00:40:31.125 --> 00:40:32.878
Activist judges, yes, Helen.

00:40:32.878 --> 00:40:34.208
- You're going to be sorry.

00:40:34.208 --> 00:40:36.458
(laughing)

00:40:40.141 --> 00:40:41.153
- What are you going to do, Helen?

00:40:41.153 --> 00:40:42.820
Ask me for a recipe?

00:40:44.819 --> 00:40:46.082
- Your decision to invade Iraq

00:40:46.082 --> 00:40:49.502
has caused the deaths of
thousands of Americans in Iraq.

00:40:49.502 --> 00:40:52.826
Wounds of Americans and
Iraqi's for a lifetime.

00:40:52.826 --> 00:40:56.090
Every reason given, publicly at least,

00:40:56.090 --> 00:40:58.824
and turned out not to be true.

00:40:58.824 --> 00:40:59.993
My question is,

00:40:59.993 --> 00:41:02.291
why did you really want to go to war?

00:41:02.291 --> 00:41:05.212
- Helen, I'm going to stop you
right there, that's enough.

00:41:05.212 --> 00:41:08.795
(everyone talking at once)

00:41:14.608 --> 00:41:16.941
Guys, no, I already said no.

00:41:20.526 --> 00:41:22.142
Guys, guys, please,
don't let Helen do this

00:41:22.142 --> 00:41:24.170
to what was a lovely day.

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:27.806
(everyone talking at once)

00:41:27.806 --> 00:41:31.556
No, no, no, no, no, I'm
not listening to you.

00:41:32.410 --> 00:41:33.431
Look what you did, Helen.

00:41:33.431 --> 00:41:34.348
I hate you.

00:41:37.036 --> 00:41:38.369
I'm out of here.

00:41:42.224 --> 00:41:44.222
There is a wall here.

00:41:44.222 --> 00:41:46.472
(laughing)

00:41:48.555 --> 00:41:50.055
It reeks in there.

00:41:52.325 --> 00:41:53.512
Ridiculous, I've never been so insulted

00:41:53.512 --> 00:41:56.722
in my entire life, stupid job.

00:41:56.722 --> 00:41:59.472
(dramatic music)

00:42:19.555 --> 00:42:20.575
Condy.

00:42:20.575 --> 00:42:23.325
(dramatic music)

00:42:38.621 --> 00:42:39.454
Help me.

00:42:40.415 --> 00:42:42.179
- [Security] There seems
to be a problem, sir.

00:42:42.179 --> 00:42:45.846
- She won't stop asking
why we invaded Iraq.

00:42:47.419 --> 00:42:49.345
- [Security] Hey, why did we invade Iraq?

00:42:49.345 --> 00:42:50.178
- No!

00:42:52.123 --> 00:42:54.873
(dramatic music)

00:43:31.698 --> 00:43:33.156
- Oh.

00:43:33.156 --> 00:43:35.461
(car beeps)

00:43:35.461 --> 00:43:38.211
(dramatic music)

00:43:54.457 --> 00:43:55.290
- No!

00:44:01.651 --> 00:44:04.484
(tires squealing)

00:44:07.465 --> 00:44:08.298
No!

00:44:14.150 --> 00:44:16.900
(dramatic music)

00:44:19.317 --> 00:44:21.900
(train engine)

00:44:30.844 --> 00:44:32.279
What a terrible trip, Danny.

00:44:32.279 --> 00:44:33.362
Take me home.

00:44:38.475 --> 00:44:39.745
- Buckle up, hon.

00:44:39.745 --> 00:44:40.578
- No!

00:44:43.891 --> 00:44:46.641
(dramatic music)

00:44:50.907 --> 00:44:53.613
Helen Thomas, ladies and gentlemen.

00:44:53.613 --> 00:44:56.613
(audience applause)

00:45:02.329 --> 00:45:04.299
Mr. Smith, members of the

00:45:04.299 --> 00:45:05.838
White House Correspondents' Association,

00:45:05.838 --> 00:45:07.547
Madam First Lady, Mr. President.

00:45:07.547 --> 00:45:10.151
It's been a true honor,
thank you very much.

00:45:10.151 --> 00:45:14.068
Good night.
(audience applause)

